 ## Proof
 
 **Proof technique:** direct planar component count.
 
-1.1 Fix $A\subseteq E$. The original faces are vertices of $G^*$. Two such faces lie in the same face of $G_A$ exactly when they are connected by dual edges corresponding to $E\setminus A$. A dual path crosses only absent primal edges and therefore stays in the complement of $G_A$. Conversely, a path between two original face points inside one face of $G_A$ can be chosen polygonal; perturb its finitely many segments to avoid primal vertices and to cross edge interiors transversely. Because it avoids $G_A$, every crossed edge lies in $E\setminus A$, so the crossed dual edges form a path in $(F(G),A^*)$. Thus the faces of $G_A$ correspond bijectively to the components counted by $k^*(A)$. [given, F2, F3, construct]
+1.1 Fix $A\subseteq E$. The drawing of $G_A$ is a finite union of points and closed line segments, hence is closed in $\mathbb R^2$. Its complement is open, so each of its face components is open and polygonally connected by [F7]. The original faces are vertices of $G^*$. Two such faces lie in the same face of $G_A$ exactly when they are connected by dual edges corresponding to $E\setminus A$. A dual path crosses only absent primal edges and therefore stays in the complement of $G_A$. Conversely, a path between two original face points inside one face of $G_A$ can be chosen polygonal; perturb its finitely many segments to avoid primal vertices and to cross edge interiors transversely. Because it avoids $G_A$, every crossed edge lies in $E\setminus A$, so the crossed dual edges form a path in $(F(G),A^*)$. Thus the faces of $G_A$ correspond bijectively to the components counted by $k^*(A)$. [given, F2, F3, F7, construct]
 
 2.1 Apply [F4] to the spanning plane subgraph $G_A$. Its vertex set is $V$, its edge set is $A$, its component count is $k(A)$, and by step 1.1 its face count is $k^*(A)$. Therefore $|V|-|A|+k^*(A)=1+k(A)$, or $k(A)-k^*(A)=|V|-|A|-1$. [F1, F4, step 1.1, algebra]
 
-2.2 View the fixed embedding on the sphere $S^2$ by adding the point at infinity. By [F4] the plane subgraph $G_A$ has finitely many faces; choose one point in each. For every vertex $x\in V$, including each vertex isolated in $G_A$, choose a closed polygonal disk $D_x$ so that the disks are pairwise disjoint and avoid all the chosen face points. Choose pairwise disjoint attachment intervals on each $\partial D_x$, one for each edge incident to $x$ and in the cyclic order of the embedding. For each $e\in A$, choose a closed polygonal rectangular band around the part of its polygonal arc outside its endpoint disks; make the bands pairwise disjoint, disjoint from all nonendpoint disks, and attach them to the corresponding intervals. These choices are possible with sufficiently small radii and widths because the graph is finite and edge arcs meet only at shared endpoints. Let $N_A$ be the union of these disks and bands. Each band joins exactly its two endpoint disks, and distinct bands otherwise remain disjoint, so the connected components of $N_A$ correspond exactly to those of $G_A$, including isolated-vertex disks; hence there are $k(A)$. Its boundary is a finite disjoint union of simple polygonal circles. In particular, the one-vertex graph with no edges gives one disk and one boundary loop. At a primal-dual crossing, the two sides of an occupied-edge band join the medial half-edges as $(h_4,h_1)$ and $(h_2,h_3)$; when the edge is absent, there is no band there and the two face-side sectors join as $(h_1,h_2)$ and $(h_3,h_4)$. The cyclic ordering of the attachment intervals fixes these local continuations, so $\partial N_A$ gives exactly the smoothings in the Statement and has $\ell(A)$ loops. Inside each face $U$ of $G_A$, the disk-and-band pieces form collars along the facial boundary side occurrences: each band side is a polygonal strip, and intervening vertex-disk sectors join consecutive strips in cyclic order. Repeated vertices or edges in a facial boundary walk are treated as separate side occurrences, so a bridge's two sides remain distinct; an isolated vertex gives a punctured-disk collar in its unique incident face. Choose all radii and widths small enough that distinct collars meet only at their prescribed continuations within the same face. Trimming them leaves a nonempty core $U_A=U\setminus N_A$, since the selected point of $U$ lies outside $N_A$. The core is connected: join any two of its points by a path in $U$, perturb the path to meet the finitely many polygonal collar sides transversely, and replace each excursion into a collar by a parallel path just outside $N_A$ along that collar's connected $U$-facing boundary, with the vertex-disk sector prescribing the continuation at each turn. Thus each face leaves exactly one nonempty component of $S^2\setminus N_A$. Since $N_A$ contains $G_A$, these trimmed faces are all the components of $S^2\setminus N_A$; by step 1.1 their number is $k^*(A)$. The regions of $S^2\setminus\partial N_A$ are the interiors of the $k(A)$ components of $N_A$ together with those $k^*(A)$ complementary components, so there are $k(A)+k^*(A)$ regions. [F2, F3, F4, given, step 1.1, construct]
